commits.body:
Item 5 of the challenge stream, on the other streams' newest rules, each built apart with its sources touched and checked against the release CLI. Nine false proofs, each Verified where a JVM says otherwise (lib/tests/fixtures/rule-countermodels, held to a JVM by lib/tests/rule_countermodels.rs; the README has a checker-side test per stream, which fails on that stream's line):
- the state stream's refusal under --gaps assume (qysmsruu) keys on the declared return class, so HashSet.clone() and Date.clone(), declared to return Object, are proved to return no HashSet and no Date (the Date case also on this line, older than the run);
- the evidence stream's binding of an observation (lzpqszyo) leaves out a supertype's static and a superinterface's array written by the initializer, a property read through Properties.get (the result says it read none), the default time zone and the environment;
- the preservation stream's WARNING-logging model (unswkkxp) takes a LogRecord subclass as confined when its getMessage writes the record the caller holds, or throws a Throwable that StreamHandler.publish lets out.
Each goes to its stream as a failure item. The state stream's I004 fix and the evidence stream's I004 binding pass their own tests. Audit: no example runs under assume; 290's five observed classes (javap on the pinned JARs) write only their own statics and read only the stated locale; 287's preservation logs through a record nothing reads after log. Nothing for the watcher.

Item 6: a mutant under controls/ for each of 287 (check-only-guard.patch: encode hashes a 73-byte password again, whose 72-byte prefix then matches), 307 (default-charset.patch: getBytes(), UTF-8 on JDK 18+, differs on 17 probes under COMPAT) and 286 (first-suppressed-only.patch: later values bound unchecked; the probes catch it, no claim does). Each is the fix's on every probe the claims cover, each claim is predicted to accept it, and each example's notes say why.
